(1,3),(2,2)\\).\n\nAny line through two of these three points has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-2}{1-2}=0,\\qquad\n\\frac{3-2}{1-2}=-1,\\qquad\n\\frac{3-2}{1-1}\\text{ undefined (vertical)}.\n\\]\n\nThus each such line is respectively horizontal, diagonal of slope \\(-1\\),\nor vertical \u2013 all non\u2011sunny. Consequently a sunny line can contain at\nmost **one** of the three uncovered points, so two sunny lines cannot\ncover all three.  \n\n*If \\(c=2\\) or \\(c=3\\):* the same argument applies after relabelling the\ncoordinates; the uncovered set again consists of three points whose\npairwise connecting lines are all non\u2011sunny. Hence the case \\(c=2,3\\)\nalso leads to a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Case 2 \u2013 \\(L_3\\) is vertical, \\(L_3:\\;x=c\\).\n\nHere \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(c=1\\):* \\(L_3\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nLines through any two of these points have slopes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-1}{2-2}\\text{ undefined (vertical)},\\qquad\n\\frac{1-1}{3-2}=0,\\qquad\n\\frac{2-1}{3-2}=-1,\n\\]\n\nagain all non\u2011sunny. Hence each sunny line can cover at most one of the\nthree uncovered points \u2013 impossible with only two sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(c=2\\) or \\(c=3\\):* the situation is symmetric and yields the same\ncontradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Case 3 \u2013 \\(L_3\\) is diagonal, \\(L_3:\\;x+y=d\\).\n\nSince the sums \\(a+b\\) for points of \\(T_3\\) are \\(2,3,4\\), we have\n\\(d\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(d=4\\):* \\(L_3\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\nAny line through two of these three points has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-1}{1-1}\\text{ undefined (vertical)},\\qquad\n\\frac{1-1}{2-1}=0,\\qquad\n\\frac{1-2}{2-1}=-1,\n\\]\n\nall non\u2011sunny. Hence again each sunny line can cover at most one of the\nthree uncovered points \u2013 impossible.\n\n*If \\(d=3\\):* \\(L_3\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nThe uncovered set is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) of size four.\nTwo sunny lines can cover at most two of these points (each sunny line\ncovers at most one, because any line joining two of them is non\u2011sunny as\nabove). Thus covering all four points is impossible.\n\n*If \\(d=2\\):* \\(L_3\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  The uncovered set has\nfive points; the same reasoning shows that two sunny lines cannot cover\nthem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible orientation of the non\u2011sunny line leads to a\ncontradiction, **no configuration of three distinct lines with exactly\ntwo sunny lines can cover all points of \\(T_3\\).**\n\nTherefore for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible. \u220e"
