\\!C\\): vertical; \\(B\\!-\\!E\\): horizontal; \\(C\\!-\\!E\\): slope \\(-1\\) |\n| 2    | \\(B,E\\)              | \\(A,C,D,F\\)      | \\(A\\!-\\!C\\): vertical; \\(A\\!-\\!D\\): horizontal; \\(A\\!-\\!F\\): horizontal; \\(C\\!-\\!D\\): slope \\(-2\\) (allowed) but then the other two remaining points \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) are each forced to be alone; see discussion below |\n| 3    | \\(C\\)                | \\(A,B,D,E,F\\)    | many pairs give slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); at least three points stay uncovered by sunny lines (see below) |\n\n*For \\(c=1\\):* the three remaining points are pairwise joined by non‑sunny lines, so a sunny line can contain at most one of them. Since three points must be covered by only two sunny lines, this is impossible.\n\n*For \\(c=2\\):* the only pair that yields an allowed slope is \\(C(1,3)\\) with \\(D(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)). Thus a sunny line could cover \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) together, leaving the set \\(\\{A,B,F\\}\\). Any line through two points of this set is either vertical (\\(A\\!-\\!B\\)) or horizontal (\\(A\\!-\\!F\\) or \\(B\\!-\\!F\\)), i.e. non‑sunny. Hence at least one of \\(A,B,F\\) would need a third sunny line – contradiction.\n\n*For \\(c=3\\):* the remaining five points contain at least three that are pairwise non‑sunny (for instance \\(A,B,D\\)). Consequently two sunny lines cannot cover all five points.\n\nThus no horizontal non‑sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Vertical non‑sunny line \\(x=c\\)\n\n| \\(c\\) | Points on \\(L_{3}\\) | Remaining points | Slopes of all pairs of remaining points |\n|------|----------------------|------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| 1    | \\(A,B,C\\)            | \\(D,E,F\\)        | \\(D\\!-\\!E\\): horizontal; \\(D\\!-\\!F\\): horizontal; \\(E\\!-\\!F\\): slope \\(-1\\) |\n| 2    | \\(D,E\\)              | \\(A,B,C,F\\)      | \\(A\\!-\\!C\\): vertical; \\(A\\!-\\!F\\): horizontal; \\(B\\!-\\!F\\): slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (allowed) but the other three points are forced to be singletons |\n| 3    | \\(F\\)                | \\(A,B,C,D,E\\)    | many pairs give slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); at least three points stay uncovered by sunny lines |\n\n*For \\(c=1\\):* the three remaining points are pairwise joined by non‑sunny lines, so each sunny line can cover at most one of them – impossible with only two sunny lines.\n\n*For \\(c=2\\):* the only admissible pair is \\(B(1,2)\\) with \\(F(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)). After covering \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) together, the points \\(\\{A,C,D\\}\\) remain; any line through two of them is vertical or horizontal, i.e. non‑sunny. Hence a third sunny line would be required – contradiction.\n\n*For \\(c=3\\):* the remaining five points contain at least three that are pairwise non‑sunny (e.g. \\(A,B,D\\)), so two sunny lines cannot suffice.\n\nThus no vertical non‑sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Diagonal non‑sunny line \\(x+y=d\\)\n\n| \\(d\\) | Points on \\(L_{3}\\) | Remaining points | Slopes of all pairs of remaining points |\n|------|----------------------|------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| 2    | \\(A\\)                | \\(B,C,D,E,F\\)    | many pairs give slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); at least three points stay uncovered |\n| 3    | \\(B,D\\)              | \\(A,C,E,F\\)      | \\(A\\!-\\!E\\): slope \\(1\\) (allowed); the other three points are pairwise non‑sunny |\n| 4    | \\(C,E,F\\)            | \\(A,B,D\\)        | \\(A\\!-\\!B\\): vertical; \\(A\\!-\\!D\\): horizontal; \\(B\\!-\\!D\\): slope \\(-1\\) |\n\n*For \\(d=2\\):* only one point lies on the diagonal, leaving five points; at least three of them are pairwise non‑sunny, so two sunny lines cannot cover all five.\n\n*For \\(d=3\\):* the admissible pair is \\(A(1,1)\\) with \\(E(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)). After covering \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) together, the remaining points are \\(\\{C,D,F\\}\\); any line through two of these is vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\). Hence a third sunny line would be needed.\n\n*For \\(d=4\\):* the three remaining points are pairwise joined by non‑sunny lines, so each sunny line can contain at most one of them – impossible with only two sunny lines.\n\nThus no diagonal non‑sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible orientation of the unique non‑sunny line leads to a situation in which the two remaining sunny lines can cover at most two of the points left uncovered by the non‑sunny line, at least one point of \\(T_{3}\\) would remain uncovered. This contradicts the requirement that **all** points of \\(T_{3}\\) be covered.\n\nTherefore for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines that covers all required points.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
