            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have solved the problem completely.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,\\;1,\\;3\\;}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Covering the left part with non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Using the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we may (and will) take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   They are allowed (vertical ∥ \\(y\\)-axis) and they cover exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n   The set of points that remain uncovered is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a translate of \\(T_k\\).  \n   Consequently any configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines yields a covering of the translated set \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and conversely a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the vertical lines (1) gives a configuration for the original problem.  \n   Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those for which the following sub‑problem is solvable.\n\n3. **Sub‑problem \\(*\\).**  \n   *For a given integer \\(m\\ge0\\), can the set \\(T_m\\) be covered by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines?*  \n\n   *Lemma 1 (small values).*  \n   \\[\n   m=0\\text{ (trivial)},\\qquad \n   m=1\\text{ – possible (any sunny line through }(1,1)),\\qquad \n   m=2\\text{ – impossible.}\n   \\]  \n   For \\(m=2\\) the three points of \\(T_2\\) are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); any line through two of them is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), all forbidden. Hence a sunny line can contain at most one point, so two sunny lines cannot cover all three points.\n\n   *Lemma 2 (the case \\(m=3\\)).*  \n   The six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y=-2x+5,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   which contain respectively \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\). Thus \\(m=3\\) is possible.\n\n   *Lemma 3 (upper bound for \\(m\\ge4\\)).*  \n   Let a sunny line intersect \\(T_m\\). Its slope cannot be \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The longest collinear subset of \\(T_m\\) with such a slope is a line of slope \\(1\\); it contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points (the points with constant difference \\(a-b\\)). Consequently any collection of \\(m\\) sunny lines covers at most  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\le m\\cdot\\frac{m}{2}\n   =\\frac{m^{2}}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points. Since \\(|T_m|=\\dfrac{m(m+1)}2\\) and \\(\\dfrac{m^{2}}2<\\dfrac{m(m+1)}2\\) for every \\(m\\ge4\\), the points of \\(T_m\\) cannot be covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines. Hence the sub‑problem \\(*\\) has a solution **iff** \\(m\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n4. **Lifting back to the original problem.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\