oints of \\(T_n\\) are at least  \n> \\[\n> |T_n|-5=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-5\\ge 1\n> \\]  \n> (the inequality holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \n> All these remaining points must be covered by the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n> Each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points, so the total capacity of the non\u2011sunny lines is \\((n-2)n\\).  \n> For \\(n\\ge3\\) we have  \n> \\[\n> (n-2)n < \\frac{n(n+1)}2-5 ,\n> \\]  \n> which is a direct computation: \u2026\u201d\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe inequality \\((n-2)n < \\frac{n(n+1)}2-5\\) is **false** for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). Indeed\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-5-(n-2)n = \\frac{-n^2+5n-10}{2}<0\n\\]\n\nfor all \\(n\\ge3\\). The correct inequality is \\((n-2)n > \\frac{n(n+1)}2-5\\). Hence the argument that the remaining points cannot be accommodated by the non\u2011sunny lines collapses. The proof that \\(k=2\\) is impossible therefore contains a fatal logical mistake and does **not** establish the claim.\n\n(Although the final statement \u201c\\(k=2\\) cannot occur\u201d happens to be true \u2013 a separate, correct proof would be needed \u2013 the presented reasoning is invalid.)\n\n---\n\n### 5. Existence constructions  \n\n#### (a) \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\). All lines are non\u2011sunny, distinct, and exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\n#### (b) \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):  \n> \\(L:\\; y-1=\\frac{1-2}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n).\\)\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  \nThe slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{-1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\); thus the line is sunny. It covers \\((n,1)\\) (the only point with \\(a=n\\)) and also \\((1,2)\\), which is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\). All other points have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and are covered by the vertical lines. The construction uses exactly one sunny line and \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n#### (c) \\(k=3\\)\n\n> \u201cThe three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_1 &: y-1 = -\\frac12\\,(x-n),\\\\\n> L_2 &: y-1 = -2\\,(x-(n-1)),\\\\\n> L_3 &: y-1 = 1\\,(x-(n-2)),\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]  \n> together cover the six points in the three right\u2011most columns, and the remaining \\(n-3\\) columns are covered by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  \nA direct substitution shows:\n\n- \\(L_1\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\);\n- \\(L_2\\) passes through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-2,3)\\);\n- \\(L_3\\) passes through \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).\n\nThus the six points \\((n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\) are covered. The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) cover all points in the remaining columns. The three slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the lines are sunny and distinct. For \\(n=3\\) the vertical part disappears and the three sunny lines alone cover the six points of \\(T_3\\). Hence the construction works for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Final collection of possibilities  \n\n> \u201cTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nBecause the bound \u201c\\(k\\le3\\)\u201d (Step\u202f3) is false and the impossibility proof for \\(k=2\\) (Step\u202f4) is invalid, the conclusion that **no** configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists has not been established. The solution therefore does **not** rigorously prove that the only possible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\). The answer is incomplete and the reasoning leading to it contains critical mistakes.\n\n---"

