            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Two elementary lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let a line have reduced rational slope \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac pq\\) with\n   \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\)\n   (i.e. the line is sunny).  \n   If the line contains two points of \\(T_n\\) then the difference of their\n   \\(x\\!+\\!y\\)‑coordinates is a non‑zero multiple of \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\); hence\n   the line contains at most  \n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   For a non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) the\n   number of points of \\(T_n\\) it can contain is at most \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Using Lemma 1 and Lemma 2, if exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are\n   sunny then the total number of incidences between the lines and the\n   points of \\(T_n\\) satisfies  \n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n+k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge |T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Solving (2) gives \\(k\\le 3\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\); consequently\n   \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Consider the three points  \n   \\[\n   P_1=(n,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_3=(n-1,2).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Any line that contains two of the points in (3) is non‑sunny:\n   \\(P_1P_2\\) is horizontal, \\(P_2P_3\\) is vertical,\n   \\(P_1P_3\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n   Hence at most one of the three points can be covered by a sunny line.\n   If exactly two sunny lines are allowed, one of them must cover a single\n   point of (3) and the other sunny line is forced to lie elsewhere.\n\n   After covering the two points of (3) that lie on a non‑sunny line,\n   the remaining uncovered points are all points of the columns\n   \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) **except possibly one point** (the point of (3)\n   covered by the sunny line).  \n   Each of those \\(n-2\\) columns contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\)\n   (because \\(n\\ge4\\)).  \n   A sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point, so the\n   single remaining sunny line can reduce the number of columns that need a\n   non‑sunny line by **at most one**.  \n   Consequently at least \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are required, but we have\n   only \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines in total (the total number of non‑sunny\n   lines is \\(n-k=n-2\\)).  The one column whose points are not covered by\n   a non‑sunny line is precisely the column that contains the point of\n   (3) covered by the sunny line; however that column still contains a\n   second point (different from the one already covered) which cannot be\n   covered by any sunny line (the only sunny line is already used).  Hence\n   at least one point of \\(T_n\\) stays uncovered – a contradiction.\n   Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).**\n\n5. **Existence constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny\n   and every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one sunny line\n   through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\); for instance the line through\n   \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\) (only for \\(n=3\\)).*  \n   Take the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) and two distinct sunny lines,\n   both passing through the point \\((2,1)\\); e.g.  \n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y-1=\\frac12\\,(x-2),\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y-1=2\\,(x-2).\n   \\]\n   The three lines are distinct, exactly two are sunny, and every point of\n   \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   For any \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y-1=-\\frac12\\,(x-n),      &&\\text{through }(n,1),(n-2,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y-1=-2\\,(x-(n-1)),        &&\\text{through }(n-1,1),(n-2,3),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y-1=   \\,(x-(n-2)),       &&\\text{through }(n-2,1),(n-1,2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Their slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so they\n   are sunny.  Together they cover exactly the six points that lie in the\n   three right‑most columns:\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-2,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3).\n   \\]\n   The remaining \\(n-3\\) columns are covered by the vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (non‑sunny).  Altogether we have \\((n-3)+3=n\\) distinct\n   lines, exactly three of them sunny.\n\n6. **Collecting the possibilities.**  \n\n   * From (2) we have \\(k\\le3\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n