oints have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and lie on the appropriate vertical line. The lines are distinct, and exactly one is sunny. Construction is correct.\n\n#### 4.3\u202f\\(k=3\\)\n- **Step:** Define the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) as in (5) and use vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  **Verification:**  \n  * Slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,-2,\\;1\\), none of which are\u202f0,\u202f\u221e,\u202f\u22121, so all three are sunny.  \n  * Direct substitution shows that each line passes through the two points listed in (6).  \n  * The six points in (6) are exactly the points of the three rightmost columns of\u202f\\(T_n\\).  \n  * The vertical lines cover every point in columns\u202f\\(1,\\dots ,n-3\\).  \n  * All lines are distinct (different slopes for the sunny lines, different \\(x\\)-coordinates for the vertical lines).  \n  Hence the construction covers all of\u202f\\(T_n\\) with exactly three sunny lines. Construction is correct.\n\n### 5. Completion of the Proof\n- The solution correctly states that the only admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) and provides valid constructions for each.  \n- However, the impossibility arguments for\u202f\\(k=2\\) and\u202f\\(k\\ge4\\) contain the justification gaps identified above. Because the solution does not supply a fully rigorous proof of these impossibilities, it does **not** meet the standard of \u201cevery step is rigorously justified.\u201d\n\n--- \n\n**Conclusion:** The answer set \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) is correct, but the proof is incomplete due to the two justification gaps in the impossibility arguments. Consequently, the solution is **not fully rigorous**."
